State-of-the-art might be a bit of an overstatement for an
architecture that's 8 years old (or even 10 if you coun't the largely
identical "plain" Series 5), but then again it might not. ;) I've got
mine since it was released and I still haven't found anything that
surpassed it in terms of usability and efficiency. Apart from playing
NetHack on it, I use it to take notes in university lectures, for
writing in general. also as an English/German dictionary, as an
encyclopedia (I have the whole text of the German Wikipedia on it), as
a mobile PDF reader (or DOC or TXT), and of course as an agenda and
contact data manager. The keyboard lets you type really fast for a
mobile device, and on top of it all, it fits nicely into the inside
pocket of my jacket, and it runs for 30+ hours on the power of two
simple AA batteries.

But I digress - actually I'm answering just because I wanted to show
you the screen size. I just made some pictures. The first one shows me
playing NetHack on the Psion. You'll see the typical way to hold the
device and to type on it when it doesn't sit on a flat surface: you
hold it in both hands and type with your thumbs, which can get very
fast with some practice. Also, notice the battery in the lower part of
the picture, this is a regular AA battery (thrown in as an
internationally recognized item for size comparison):

http://www.uss-pearson.de/Psion/size.jpg

Here's another one that shows the screen a little larger (with the
battery right next to it). Note that the screen is much clearer in
reality than it appears on the picture:

http://www.uss-pearson.de/Psion/Screen.jpg

And here's the (probably failed) attempt to show the font of the Psion
port; note the boulder in the left part of the screen, which really
looks like a boulder and not like an apostrophe (which *would* be hard
to recognize an a Psion). Also, fountains really look like fountains
(none to show in my current game, unfortunately):

http://www.uss-pearson.de/Psion/screen-extract.jpg

You can get a much better impression from Duncan Booth's page, which
also shows a fountain:

http://www.suttoncourtenay.org.uk/duncan/Nethack.htm/Pictures

(although this page shows screenshots from the emulator, not pictures
from the game running on the machine itself)

Anyway, my point is that the Psion is probably better suited for
playing NetHack than most people expect. If you want a small, light,
and very efficient mobile device to play NetHack on, get a Psion, used
ones are cheap nowadays. :)


